      "text": "Given $z = r e^{i\\theta}$, then we can rewrite the given equation as $r^{28}e^{28i\\theta} - r^{8}e^{8i\\theta} - 1 = 0$. \n\nUpon examination, the magnitude $m$ of this complex number must be 1, so we have $r^{28} - 1 = 0$. Solving for $r$, we get $r = 1$.\n\nSince we are given $|z| = 1$, $z$ lies on the unit circle in the complex plane.\n\nNow, because the angles $\\theta_{m}$ satisfy $m \\theta_{m} = \\theta_{1}, 2\\theta_{1}, 3\\theta_{1}, \\dots, (28 - 1)\\theta_{1}$, the angles $\\theta_{1}, \\theta_{2}, \\dots, \\theta_{2n}$ represent the vertices of a regular 28-gon centered at the origin.\n\nTo find $\\sum\\theta_{m}$, we use the formula for the sum of angles in a regular polygon: $\\theta_{m} = \\frac{\\pi m}{28}$. \n\nThe desired sum then becomes $\\frac{\\pi \\sum m}{28}$, which is the same as $\\boxed{\\frac{29\\pi}{28}}$. \n\nThe final answer is \\frac{29\\pi}{28}.",
