      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "Given the equations $z^{28} - z^{8} - 1 = 0$ and $|z| = 1$, we know that $z = \\cos\\theta + i\\sin\\theta$ from De Moivre's Theorem.\n\nRearranging the first equation, we get $z^{8}(z^{20} - 1) = 1$. Then, $(\\cos\\theta)^{8}(\\cos(20\\theta) - \\sin(20\\theta) + i(\\cos(20\\theta) + \\sin(20\\theta))) = 1 + 0i$ from the sum $e^{i\\theta} + e^{-i\\theta} = 2\\cos\\theta$.\n\nSimplifying, $\\cos(20\\theta) - \\sin(20\\theta) = \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cos(20\\theta)$, leading to $\\sin(20\\theta) = \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cos(20\\theta)$. Since both sine and cosine can't be 0 at the same time for non-null angles, the only way to satisfy this equation is through special angles.\n\nThus, $20\\theta = 30^\\circ$ or $30^\\circ + 180^\\circ = 150^\\circ$ or $-30^\\circ$, giving us $\\theta_{8} = 30^\\circ$, $\\theta_{18} = 30^\\circ$, and $\\theta_{12} = -30^\\circ$. Grouping the angles in ascending order gives us $\\begin{aligned}\n\\theta_{0} = 0 \\\\\n\\theta_{1} = 30^\\circ \\\\\n\\theta_{2} = 60^\\circ \\\\\n\\theta_{3} = 90^\\circ \\\\\n\\theta_{4} = 120^\\circ \\\\\n\\theta_{5} = 150^\\circ \\\\\n\\theta_{6} = 180^\\circ \\\\\n\\theta_{7} = 210^\\circ \\\\\n\\theta_{8} = 240^\\circ \\\\\n\\theta_{9} = 270^\\circ \\\\\n\\theta_{10} = 300^\\circ \\\\\n\\theta_{11} = 330^\\circ\n\\end{aligned}$. Therefore, $\\theta_{2} + \\theta_{4} + \\dots + \\theta_{2n} = 5\\cdot\\frac{(210 + 240)}{2} - (240 + 180 + 150 + 90 + 60 + 30) = 300$.\n\nThus, the value is $\\boxed{300}$.",
