      "text": "Let the $28$-th roots of unity be $\\zeta^{k} = \\cos 0 + i\\sin 0$, where $k = 0, 1, 2, \\dots, 27$.\n\nAdding $\\zeta^{30} = \\cos (-360^{\\circ}) + i\\sin 0^{\\circ}$ to both sides of the given equation, we have \n\\[\\zeta^{30} + z_1 + z_2 + \\dots + z_{28} = 2\\]\n\\[\\tan 0^{\\circ} + \\tan \\theta_1 + \\dots + \\tan \\theta_{14} = 2\\]\n\nLet's rewrite the second expression. Since the tangent of an angle is equal to the ratio of the sine of the angle to the cosine of the angle, we can rewrite the expression as:\n\\[\\cos 0 + \\tan \\theta_1 \\cos^2 \\theta_1 + \\dots + \\cos 360 \\tan^2 \\theta_{14} \\cos^2 \\theta_{14} = 2\\]\n\\[\\cos 0 + \\frac{\\sin^2 \\theta_1 + \\dots + \\tan^2 \\theta_{14} \\cos^2 \\theta_{14}}{\\sin \\theta_1 + \\dots + \\tan \\theta_{14} \\cos \\theta_{14}} = 2\\]\n\\[\\cos 0 + \\frac{\\sin^2 \\theta_1 + \\dots + \\tan^2 \\theta_{14} \\cos^2 \\theta_{14}}{\\sin \\theta_1 + \\dots + \\tan \\theta_{14} \\cos \\theta_{14}} = 2\\]\n\nSince $\\theta_1, \\dots, \\theta_{14} \\in [0, 360)$ and $\\theta_1 + \\dots + \\theta_{14} \\in [0, 360)$, we have\n\\[\\cos 0 + \\frac{\\sin^2 \\theta_1 + \\dots + \\tan^2 \\theta_{14} \\cos^2 \\theta_{14}}{\\sin \\theta_1 + \\dots + \\tan \\theta_{14} \\cos \\theta_{14}} = 2\\]\n\\[\\frac{1 + \\sin^2 \\theta_1 + \\dots + \\tan^2 \\theta_{14} \\cos^2 \\theta_{14}}{\\sin \\theta_1 + \\dots + \\tan \\theta_{14} \\cos \\theta_{14}} = 2\\]\n\\[\\sin \\theta_1 + \\dots + \\tan \\theta_{14} \\cos \\theta_{14} + \\sin^2 \\theta_1 + \\dots + \\tan^2 \\theta_{14} \\cos^2 \\theta_{14} = 2\\]\n\nTherefore, we have $\\sin \\theta_1 + \\dots + \\tan \\theta_{14} \\cos \\theta_{14} + \\sin^2 \\theta_1 + \\dots + \\tan^2 \\theta_{14} \\cos^2 \\theta_{14} = 2$, which means that \n\\[\\cos \\theta_1 + \\dots + \\cot \\theta_{14} \\sin \\theta_{14} + \\sin^2 \\theta_1 + \\dots + \\tan^2 \\theta_{14} \\cos^2 \\theta_{14} = 2\\]\n\\[\\cos \\theta_1 + \\dots + \\cot \\theta_{14} \\sin \\theta_{14} = 0\\]\n\nTherefore, we have $\\theta_1, \\dots, \\theta_{14} \\in (-90^{\\circ}, 90^{\\circ})$, the angles are equally spaced. Hence, $\\theta_1 + \\dots + \\theta_{14} = \\boxed{0}$ radians. The answer is: 0",
